---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import BiodiversitySim from '../../simulators/biodiversity/BiodiversitySim.astro';
import { biodiversityQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/biodiversity';

const toc = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'What is biodiversity?' },
  { id: 'stability', label: 'Diversity and stability' },
  { id: 'experiment', label: 'The experiment behind the model' },
  { id: 'services', label: 'Why biodiversity matters to people' },
  { id: 'keystone', label: 'Keystone species' },
  { id: 'threats', label: 'Threats to biodiversity' },
  { id: 'protect', label: 'Protecting biodiversity' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Tilman, D., Reich, P. B., & Knops, J. M. H. (2006). Biodiversity and ecosystem stability in a decade-long grassland experiment. Nature, 441, 629–632.', url: 'https://doi.org/10.1038/nature04742' },
  { text: 'Isbell, F., et al. (2015). Biodiversity increases the resistance of ecosystem productivity to climate extremes. Nature, 526, 574–577.', url: 'https://doi.org/10.1038/nature15374' },
  { text: 'Yachi, S., & Loreau, M. (1999). Biodiversity and ecosystem productivity in a fluctuating environment: the insurance hypothesis. PNAS, 96(4), 1463–1468.' },
  { text: 'IPBES (2019). Global Assessment Report on Biodiversity and Ecosystem Services: Summary for Policymakers.', url: 'https://www.ipbes.net/global-assessment' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="ecology"
  topic="biodiversity"
  description="Interactive biodiversity experiment: compare grassland plots with 1, 4 and many species as droughts, heatwaves and disease strike, and see why diverse ecosystems are more stable."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={biodiversityQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Does it matter how many species live in an ecosystem? Run an experiment that ecologists have carried out for real:
      grow three meadows with different numbers of plant species, hit them with the same drought, heatwave or disease, and
      compare how well each one copes and recovers.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <BiodiversitySim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Let the three plots grow. Note that the mixed plots end up slightly more productive than the monoculture.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Drought</strong>, <strong>Heatwave</strong> or <strong>Ryegrass disease</strong>. All three plots are hit at the same time.</li>
      <li>Compare how far each plot’s growth falls (<strong>resistance</strong>) and how quickly it recovers (<strong>resilience</strong>) in the readouts and graph.</li>
      <li>Watch which species survive and spread in the diverse plots while others suffer.</li>
      <li>Change the number of species in plot C and repeat. Is there a point where adding more species makes little difference?</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="what">What is biodiversity?</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Biodiversity</strong> is the variety of life on Earth. It is usually described at three levels:
    <strong>genetic diversity</strong> (differences between individuals of the same species), <strong>species
    diversity</strong> (how many different species there are and how evenly they are spread), and <strong>ecosystem
    diversity</strong> (the range of habitats, from coral reefs to grasslands). Scientists have described about 2 million
    species, but there may be 8 million or more.
  </p>

  <h2 id="stability">Diversity and stability</h2>
  <p>
    Ecosystems are constantly disturbed by droughts, storms, pests and diseases. In a diverse community, species react
    differently: when a drought kills a shallow-rooted grass, a deep-rooted plant or a drought-tolerant clover may survive
    and expand into the space. Total plant growth stays more stable even though individual species rise and fall. This
    is known as the <strong>insurance hypothesis</strong>: the more species there are, the more likely it is that some
    will cope with whatever happens.
  </p>

  <h2 id="experiment">The experiment behind the model</h2>
  <p>
    At Cedar Creek in Minnesota, ecologist David Tilman and colleagues planted 168 grassland plots with between 1 and 16
    species and measured them for decades. Plots with more species produced more plant growth and varied much less from
    year to year. During severe droughts, diverse plots lost far less productivity and recovered faster. Similar results
    have since been found in many experiments around the world.
  </p>

  <h2 id="services">Why biodiversity matters to people</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Food security:</strong> crop diversity and wild relatives protect against pests and diseases. The Irish potato famine of the 1840s struck a country depending on very few potato varieties.</li>
    <li><strong>Pollination:</strong> about three quarters of food crops benefit from animal pollinators.</li>
    <li><strong>Clean water and air, fertile soils and flood control</strong> all depend on healthy ecosystems.</li>
    <li><strong>Medicines:</strong> many drugs, from aspirin to cancer treatments, come from wild organisms.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="keystone">Keystone species</h2>
  <p>
    Some species matter far more than their numbers suggest. Sea otters eat sea urchins; without otters, urchins strip
    kelp forests bare. Beavers build dams that create wetlands used by hundreds of other species. Losing a
    <strong>keystone species</strong> can cause an entire ecosystem to change.
  </p>

  <h2 id="threats">Threats to biodiversity</h2>
  <p>
    The main causes of biodiversity loss today are <strong>habitat loss</strong> from farming and building,
    <strong>overexploitation</strong> through overfishing and hunting, <strong>climate change</strong>,
    <strong>pollution</strong> and <strong>invasive species</strong>. Wildlife populations monitored worldwide have fallen
    by around two thirds on average since 1970, and about a million species are estimated to be at risk of extinction.
  </p>

  <h2 id="protect">Protecting biodiversity</h2>
  <p>
    Protected areas, habitat restoration, wildlife corridors that link habitats, sustainable farming and fishing,
    controlling invasive species, and seed banks all help. Even small actions, such as leaving wildflower patches for
    pollinators, add up.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Monoculture</dt>
    <dd>Growing a single species over a large area.</dd>
    <dt>Resistance / resilience</dt>
    <dd>How little an ecosystem changes during a disturbance / how quickly it recovers afterwards.</dd>
    <dt>Ecosystem services</dt>
    <dd>The benefits people get from nature, such as food, clean water and pollination.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Biodiversity just means lots of animals.”</strong> It includes plants, fungi, microbes, genes and habitats.</li>
    <li><strong>“Losing one species doesn’t matter.”</strong> Each loss removes a role and a backup in the ecosystem; keystone losses can be dramatic.</li>
    <li><strong>“Monocultures are always more productive.”</strong> Diverse systems often match or exceed them, and are far more stable.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Biodiversity is the variety of genes, species and ecosystems.</li>
    <li>Diverse ecosystems resist disturbances better and recover faster.</li>
    <li>Different species use resources in different ways, often making diverse systems more productive.</li>
    <li>Habitat loss is the leading cause of biodiversity decline.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: species traits are invented for the demonstration and the plots are simplified. The pattern, more stability with more species, matches long-term field experiments.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
